#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# Calculus and transforms

#% text
# `limit`, `d`, `series`, `integrate` and the integral transforms work symbolically; the numerical counterparts (`n_integrate`, `n_sum`) take over when no closed form exists.

#% section
# Derivatives and limits

#% cell
d(x^3*sin(x), x)
#% output
#= cos(x)*x^3 + 3*x^2*sin(x)

#% text
# `d(f, [x, 2])` is the second derivative.

#% cell
d(exp(-x^2), [x, 2])
#% output
#= -2*exp(-x^2) + 4*exp(-x^2)*x^2

#% cell
limit(sin(x)/x, x -> 0)
#% output
#= 1

#% text
# A two-sided limit that does not exist is reported, and `direction:` names one side.

#% cell
limit(1/x, x -> 0, direction: from_above)
#% output
#= inf

#% cell
limit((1 + 1/n)^n, n -> inf)
#% output
#= e

#% section
# Series and sums

#% text
# `series(e, x, x0, k)` expands about `x0` up to the power `k`; the remainder is written `O(...)`.

#% cell
series(exp(x), x, 0, 5)
#% output
#= 1 + x + x^2/2 + x^3/6 + x^4/24 + x^5/120 + O(x^6)

#% cell
series(1/(1 - x), x, 0, 4)
#% output
#= 1 + x + x^2 + x^3 + x^4 + O(x^5)

#% cell
sum(1/k^2, [k, 1, inf])
#% output
#= (1/6)*pi^2

#% cell
sum(k^2, [k, 1, n])
#% output
#= (1/6)*n*(1 + n)*(1 + 2*n)

#% section
# Integration

#% text
# `integrate(f, x)` is an antiderivative, `integrate(f, [x, a, b])` a definite integral. An integral for which no closed form is found stays as it is; `n_integrate` computes it numerically.

#% cell
integrate(x*exp(x), x)
#% output
#= -exp(x) + x*exp(x)

#% cell
integrate(1/(1 + x^2), [x, 0, 1])
#% output
#= (1/4)*pi

#% cell
integrate(exp(-x^2), [x, -inf, inf])
#% output
#= pi^(1/2)

#% cell
integrate(exp(-x^2), [x, 0, 1])
#% output
#= (1/2)*erf(1)*pi^(1/2)

#% cell
integrate(x^x, [x, 0, 1])
#% output
#= integrate(x^x, [x, 0, 1])

#% cell
n_integrate(x^x, [x, 0, 1])
#% output
#= 0.7834305107121345

#% section
# Integral transforms

#% text
# `laplace_transform(f, t, s)`, `fourier_transform(f, t, w)`, `mellin_transform(f, t, s)` and `z_transform(f, n, z)` have inverses `inverse_laplace_transform` and so on. The result is a function of the new variable.

#% cell
laplace_transform(exp(-2t)*sin(3t), t, s)
#% output
#= 3/(9 + (2 + s)^2)

#% cell
inverse_laplace_transform(1/(s^2 + 1), s, t)
#% output
#= sin(t)

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-t^2), t, w)
#% output
#= (1/2)*2^(1/2)*exp((-1/4)*w^2)

#% cell
z_transform(2^n, n, z)
#% output
#= z/(-2 + z)

#% section
# Common mistakes

#% item
# The variable comes second in `d(f, x)`. A product needs `*`: `d(x(x + 1), x)` differentiates a call of `x`, not the product `x*(x + 1)`.

#% cell
d(x(x + 1), x)
#% output
#= x'(1 + x)

#% cell
d(x*(x + 1), x)
#% output
#= 1 + 2*x

#% item
# `limit(f, x -> a)` takes a rule, not a list. A limit whose two sides differ has no two-sided value (`failure(no_limit, ...)`): name a side with `direction:`.

#% cell
limit(1/x, x -> 0)
#% output
#! failure(no_limit, limit, x^-1, "the two-sided limit does not exist: the function is unbounded, tending to inf on one side and -inf on the other")

#% cell
limit(1/x, x -> 0, direction: from_below)
#% output
#= -inf

#% item
# A range that passes a singularity is not integrated blindly: `integrate(1/x^2, [x, -1, 1])` diverges and says so. An `integrate(...)` that comes back unchanged means instead that no closed form was found.

#% cell
integrate(1/x^2, [x, -1, 1])
#% output
#! failure(divergent, integrate, x^-2)

#% section
# Related functions

#% text
# `d`, `limit`, `series`, `integrate`, `n_integrate`, `sum`, `laplace_transform`, `fourier_transform`, `z_transform`, `mellin_transform`
